---
// Documents the per-dataset private-holdout split (20–40% private) enforced
// by `synthbench.private_holdout` and surfaced in the leaderboard as the
// "verified" / "flagged" verification badge.
---

<section class="w-full max-w-4xl mx-auto py-12" id="private-holdout">
  <h2
    class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-text dark:text-text-dark mb-2"
  >
    Private holdout split
  </h2>
  <p class="text-muted mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    Every holdout-enabled dataset is partitioned deterministically into a
    public subset and a private subset. The private fraction is per-dataset,
    sized to preserve statistical power: 20% for large datasets (OpinionsQA,
    WVS, GSS), 30% for medium ones (SubPOP, GlobalOpinionQA), and 40% for
    small ones (Michigan, NTIA, Eurobarometer). Per-question human
    distributions for the private subset are suppressed from every public
    SynthBench artifact — the site, the run-detail JSON, and the
    question-explorer pages — so a submitter can't recover the private
    answer key by reading our published files.
  </p>
  <p class="text-muted mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    Submissions must include results for every question, public and
    private. We (the SynthBench maintainers) score the private subset
    locally against the withheld answer key and publish the resulting
    <span class="font-mono">sps_private</span> alongside
    <span class="font-mono">sps_public</span>. Rows whose public/private
    SPS delta stays within a tolerance of 0.05 earn a
    <span
      class="mx-1 inline-block text-xs px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400"
      >✓ verified</span
    > badge; rows outside the tolerance are
    <span
      class="mx-1 inline-block text-xs px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-amber-500/10 text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400"
      >⚠ flagged</span
    > for review.
  </p>
  <p class="text-muted mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    The partition is derived from an unsalted SHA-256 hash of <span
      class="font-mono">dataset_name + ":" + question_key</span
    >. That makes it stable across runs, machines, and Python versions
    without needing a seed file. It also means <strong
      >which questions are private is not a secret</strong
    >: the hash is public code, run artifacts carry explicit
    <span class="font-mono">is_holdout</span> flags, and anyone can compute
    any question's membership. That is deliberate and Kaggle-like — what is
    withheld is the private subset's <em>answer key</em> (the per-question
    human distributions), not the list of held-out questions.
  </p>
  <p class="text-muted mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    <strong>Honest limits.</strong> The source surveys behind these datasets
    are publicly downloadable upstream, and our open-source adapters will
    rebuild the full per-question distributions — private subset included —
    for anyone willing to run them. A determined actor can therefore
    reconstruct the private targets. The holdout is a fabrication detector
    and a deterrent against casual overfitting, not cryptographic secrecy:
    it raises the effort bar from "copy our published JSON" to "reprocess
    the upstream source". A genuinely un-memorizable holdout would require
    newly fielded human data on a rotation cadence — a data-acquisition
    program scoped separately from this mechanism.
  </p>
  <p class="text-muted max-w-3xl">
    <strong>Why bother.</strong> Two independent motivations converge on
    the same mechanism. First, future LLMs may train on SynthBench itself;
    holding out 20–40% per dataset slows that recursion. Second (and more
    important after
    the public flip): once per-question human distributions are visible,
    a bad actor could fabricate a submission JSON that exactly matches
    them. The private subset is a cheap cheating detector — a fabricator
    has no signal on the hidden keys, so their public and private SPS
    diverge sharply.
  </p>

  <div id="verification-badges" class="mt-8 max-w-3xl scroll-mt-24">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-text dark:text-text-dark mb-2">
      Reading the verified / flagged badges
    </h3>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      Every leaderboard row on a holdout-enabled dataset carries one of two
      badges, computed at publish time by comparing the row's SPS on the
      public subset against its SPS on the private subset
      (<span class="font-mono">|sps_public − sps_private|</span>, the
      published <span class="font-mono">sps_public_private_delta</span>):
    </p>
    <ul class="text-muted mb-4 space-y-3 list-none pl-0">
      <li>
        <span
          class="mr-1 inline-block text-xs px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400"
          >✓ verified</span
        > — the public and private scores agree to within the divergence
        threshold of 0.05 SPS. This is the expected behavior of an honest
        run: typical honest-submission deltas we've observed are below
        0.02.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span
          class="mr-1 inline-block text-xs px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-amber-500/10 text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400"
          >⚠ flagged</span
        > — the public/private divergence exceeded the 0.05 threshold, so
        the row is held for review.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      <strong>What a flag means — and doesn't.</strong> A flag is a
      statistical anomaly detector, not proof of cheating. A large
      divergence is what fabrication looks like (a fabricator can match
      the public answer key but has no signal on the hidden one), but it
      can also arise from overfitting to the public subset or, on datasets
      with a small private subset, from ordinary sampling noise
      concentrated in a handful of questions. Treat a flagged score with
      caution — prefer the row's <span class="font-mono">sps_private</span>
      when deciding how much to trust it — rather than reading the flag as
      an accusation against the submitter.
    </p>
    <p class="text-muted">
      The threshold is deliberately loose (0.05, versus typical honest
      deltas under 0.02) so that normal run-to-run noise does not flag
      honest submissions. Both scores and the delta are published on each
      row, so you can apply a stricter or looser standard yourself.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
